## API Security – A Practical Checklist

Below is a “cook‑book” of best practices that cover the whole API lifecycle: **design → implementation → deployment → maintenance**.  Use it as a living reference and adapt it to your organization’s risk profile, regulatory requirements, and tooling stack.

| Category | Best Practice | Why it matters | Quick win |
|----------|---------------|----------------|-----------|
| **Design** | 1️⃣ Threat modeling (OWASP API Security 2024) | Identify data flows, trust boundaries, and potential attack vectors before coding | Use a simple “canvas” or a tool like Microsoft Threat Modeling Tool |
| | 2️⃣ Principle of least privilege | Reduce blast radius of a compromised credential | Grant only the scopes needed for each client |
| | 3️⃣ Enforce API versioning & deprecation strategy | Avoid breaking clients & keep security controls in sync | `Accept: application/vnd.company.v2+json` |
| | 4️⃣ Separate public & private APIs | Minimize exposure of sensitive endpoints | Place private APIs behind a VPC or an API gateway |
| **Authentication & Authorization** | 5️⃣ Use OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect | Standard, auditable, token‑based auth | Adopt PKCE for mobile & SPA |
| | 6️⃣ Use short‑lived access tokens & rotating refresh tokens | Limits time‑of‑use for stolen tokens | Refresh tokens >30 min, access tokens <5 min |
| | 7️⃣ Enforce scopes & audience checks | Prevent privilege escalation | Validate `aud` and `scp` on every call |
| | 8️⃣ Mutual TLS (mTLS) for critical services | Strong client authentication & encryption | Use AWS IoT Core mTLS or Istio side‑car |
| | 9️⃣ Multi‑factor authentication for admin APIs | Extra layer of protection | 2FA via Google Authenticator or FIDO2 |
| **Transport & Data** | 🔟 TLS 1.2+ everywhere | Encrypt data in transit | Enforce TLS via API gateway or load balancer |
| | 1️⃣1️⃣ Disable HTTP/1.1 keep‑alive for public endpoints | Prevent certain DoS patterns | `Connection: close` |
| | 1️⃣2️⃣ Use content security policy (CSP) & CORS properly | Reduce XSS & CSRF on browsers | `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://trusted.com` |
| | 1️⃣3️⃣ Encrypt sensitive payload fields | Even if TLS is broken, data stays safe | FPE or field‑level encryption |
| **Input Validation & Sanitization** | 1️⃣4️⃣ Strict schema validation (OpenAPI/JSON‑Schema) | Guard against injection & malformed data | Use libraries like Ajv, jsonschema‑validator |
| | 1️⃣5️⃣ Rate limiting & throttling | Thwart brute‑force & DoS | `X-RateLimit-Limit: 1000` & `Retry‑After` |
| | 1️⃣6️⃣ Use whitelists, not blacklists | Reduce false negatives | Validate against enumerated allowed values |
| | 1️⃣7️⃣ Guard against XML/JSON parsing attacks | Prevent XXE, DOS via large payloads | Set parser limits (size, depth) |
| **Logging & Monitoring** | 1️⃣8️⃣ Log all auth & request events | Forensics & detection | Store logs in a write‑once, tamper‑evident store |
| | 1️⃣9️⃣ Implement real‑time alerts (SIEM) | Early breach detection | Alert on abnormal IP, repeated failures, token abuse |
| | 2️⃣0️⃣ Correlate logs across services | Easier root‑cause analysis | Use structured logs (JSON) & common correlation ID |
| **Testing & Validation** | 2️⃣1️⃣ Unit & integration tests for auth flows | Avoid regressions | Mock OAuth server or use `oauth2-client` library |
| | 2️⃣2️⃣ Automated security scanning (SAST, SCA, DAST) | Early defect discovery | GitHub CodeQL, Snyk, OWASP ZAP |
| | 2️⃣3️⃣ Regular penetration tests & red‑team | Verify real‑world attack resilience | Schedule bi‑annual or after major changes |
| | 2️⃣4️⃣ API vulnerability database integration | Stay updated on CVEs | Sync with NVD, MITRE, or commercial feeds |
| **Secrets Management** | 2️⃣5️⃣ Store secrets in a vault (HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager) | No hard‑coded credentials | Use dynamic secrets that rotate automatically |
| | 2️⃣6️⃣ Rotate keys & certificates regularly | Mitigate long‑term exposure | Automated rotation scripts |
| | 2️⃣7️⃣ Use least‑privilege for service accounts | Minimize damage if compromised | Service‑to‑service IAM roles |
| **Governance & Compliance** | 2️⃣8️⃣ Define an API security policy & enforce via CI/CD | Consistent security posture | Gate commits with static checks |
| | 2️⃣9️⃣ Keep audit trails for regulatory compliance | Demonstrate due diligence | Log all changes to API schema |
| | 3️⃣0️⃣ Adopt industry frameworks (NIST SP 800‑53, ISO 27001) | Align with best‑in‑class standards | Map controls to your API security checklist |

